-- title:
--   The energy over ℝ^d is the supremum of the energies over balls
-- statement:
--   For **any** kernel $k$ and **any** $f$, with no hypotheses at all,
--
--   $$\mathcal E_{\mathbb{R}^d}[k,f] \;=\; \sup_{n \in \mathbb{N}} \mathcal E_{B(0,\,n+1)}[k,f].$$
--
--   Both sides are lower Lebesgue integrals in $[0,\infty]$, so no finiteness or measurability is needed:
--   the balls $B(0,n+1)$ increase to $\mathbb{R}^d$ and monotone convergence does the rest.
--
--   It is the step that turns a ball statement into a statement on all of $\mathbb{R}^d$ — the source uses
--   exactly this to pass from Lemma 3.7 to Theorem 1.4, "by letting $R \to \infty$", which is legitimate
--   precisely because the constant there does not depend on the ball.
-- source:
--   https://github.com/dbenbenn/quadratic-forms-sobolev/blob/cc13121ff7b667aa31d9a3cd85088f5575d56ca5/QuadraticFormsSobolev/Section1.lean#L258-L263

